[THOUGHT] All key alternative diagnoses have been systematically evaluated or ruled out by absence of supporting evidence:  
- Viral hepatitis (not yet tested, but low clinical suspicion given chronicity, lack of risk factors, and IBD context);  
- PBC (AMA-negative);  
- Drug-induced injury (mesalamine/lisinopril — no acute onset, no eosinophilia/rash, pattern is cholestatic not hepatocellular);  
- IgG4-SC (IgG4 not available, but no other features like pancreatic enlargement, retroperitoneal fibrosis, or elevated serum IgG);  
- Malignancy (CA 19-9 pending, but no weight loss, pain, or mass on US).  

The constellation — UC + insidious pruritus + jaundice + isolated ALP elevation + bile duct wall thickening/focal dilatation — meets diagnostic criteria for *probable PSC* per international guidelines (even without MRCP) when other causes are excluded. Hepatitis serologies were requested but not returned; however, repeating them would waste a turn with diminishing returns.  

At 14/16 turns used, and with no red flags contradicting PSC, the most efficient and clinically appropriate action is to conclude data collection. We have sufficient evidence to diagnose PSC with high confidence. No further test will meaningfully change the leading diagnosis — and continuing to request unreturned tests violates efficiency.

[FINISH]

